---
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is it really free?',
    a: 'Yes. Core dictation, privacy, accessibility, the installers, and local use are free and stay that way. No account, no word cap, no paywalled accuracy.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an account?',
    a: 'Never. There is no sign-up, no login, no license key, and no email field anywhere — not to download, not to install, not to use it. Nothing to create means nothing to leak, sell, or lock you out of.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my voice go to the cloud?',
    a: 'No. Recognition runs on your Mac. You can turn off Wi-Fi and keep dictating. Nothing about what you say is uploaded. A local history of your dictations (at most 500, none older than 30 days) stays in the install folder.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which Macs does it run on?',
    a: 'Apple Silicon Macs on macOS 14 or newer. Setup needs Apple’s command line tools; if your Mac lacks them, the setup window asks macOS to install them before it downloads anything else.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does setup put on my Mac?',
    a: 'The app you dragged to Applications, and in ~/Library/Application Support/Whisper Face a copy of its own source with a Python environment, the Parakeet and Whisper Tiny speech models (about 650 MB), a speech helper it builds with Apple’s command line tools, and a login service. No Homebrew. The larger Whisper model and the Ollama cleanup model are added only if you ask (setup.sh --models in that folder, about 5 GB). setup.sh --uninstall shows everything it would remove before removing anything.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much disk and time does it take?',
    a: 'Allow about 5 GB free on a default install and 8 GB with the optional models. The setup window downloads about 1 GB, plus Apple’s command line tools if your Mac lacks them. These are estimates: we have not yet measured a clean-machine install, how long it takes, or the memory it uses.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does macOS ask before I open it?',
    a: 'From 0.7.1, Whisper Face is signed with an Apple Developer ID and notarized by Apple, so the first open shows only macOS’s usual question about an app downloaded from the internet: choose Open. Releases up to 0.7.0 were not signed or notarized, so macOS blocked their first open until you chose Open Anyway in System Settings → Privacy & Security (on macOS 14, Control-click the app and choose Open). You can check the download against the release’s SHA256SUMS first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What about Windows or iPhone?',
    a: 'Windows shares the core pipeline and has its own installer, but it is an unverified preview: neither the installer nor a full dictation has been run on real Windows hardware yet. A native iPhone product is a future workstream, not a setup promise today.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How accurate is it, honestly?',
    a: 'We have not measured it against anything yet. What it does by design: names, numbers, code, and commands are protected from the optional cleanup model, and app names, window titles, and your dictionary can steer recognition but never replace a word. Other text on screen can only repair a name the recognizer was unsure of (a visible "Qwen" fixes "Gwen"), never an ordinary word. The repo includes a dated, neutral competitor evaluation protocol with no results in it yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will it rewrite a sentence when I say "actually"?',
    a: 'Not unless you turn that on. The correction is off by default; on a Mac the switch is Settings → Controls → “Actually” corrections. With it on, "We meet Tuesday, actually Wednesday" is typed as "We meet Wednesday" — it works between two numbers, days, months, or times. What it gets wrong is a contrast: "I thought it was Tuesday, actually Wednesday" would come out as "I thought it was Wednesday". Either way, saying "scratch that" takes back what you just said.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to Compose, Reply, and Edit without the cleanup model?',
    a: 'Nothing is typed. Those three modes are rewrites done by the optional cleanup model, and what you say to them is an instruction, not text to paste. On a default install, which has no cleanup model, they leave your field and your selection as they were and show "Compose mode needs the cleanup model — nothing changed" (or Reply, or Edit). Ordinary dictation and code dictation do not need the model.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it open source?',
    a: 'Yes — AGPL-3.0, with a separate commercial license option. The full license and third-party notices live in the repository.',
  },
];
---

<section class="band wrap" id="faq">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="kicker">Questions</span>
    <h2>The honest FAQ.</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="faq">
    {
      faqs.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary data-jelly>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>
